  "client_view_text": " The three-way handshake is a process used by the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) to establish a connection between a client and a server. It is called a three-way handshake because it involves three exchanges of data between the client and server. The three-way handshake is necessary to ensure that both the client and server are ready to communicate and to establish a reliable connection. Here's how it works: 1. The client sends a SYN (synchronize) packet to the server. This packet contains the client's initial sequence number (ISN) and other information necessary for the connection. The SYN packet is sent to the server's IP address and port number. 2. The server receives the SYN packet and responds with a SYN-ACK (synchronize-acknowledgment) packet. This packet contains the server's ISN and an acknowledgment of the client's ISN. The server also sets the sequence number to the next expected sequence number. 3. The client receives the SYN-ACK packet and responds with an ACK (acknowledgment) packet. This packet contains an acknowledgment of the server's ISN and the sequence number set by the server. The client also sets the sequence number to the next expected sequence number. The three-way handshake is necessary for several reasons:"
}